  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 252 AM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nOverall relatively quiet weather is expected over the next week,\nthough there are a few things to mention. First, weak WAA with\nlimited mid-level moisture is helping to produce widespread\nradar echoes early this morning across central North Dakota. \nHowever, with very dry air near the surface and an increasingly \nwarm inversion, wouldn't expect much beyond a few sprinkles \nreaching the ground. Winds are relatively light early this \nmorning across most of the area. However, downslope enhancement \noff the Missouri Coteau is leading to breezy conditions right \nalong the slope. These winds should weaken later this morning \nwith most of the state seeing mostly sunny skies, light winds, \nand highs in the low 40s to low 50s for Thanksgiving.\n\nFor Friday, surface low pressure will organize and slide from \nwest to east across southern Canada. An associated weak cold \nfrontal or wind shift boundary will pass through the state, with\nlight rain possible ahead of the front. Generally speaking, \ntemperatures are favored to remain above freezing while \nprecipitation is falling, thus resulting in mostly rain. \nHowever, a little freezing rain is possible, especially in areas\nwhere skies stay mostly clear tonight ahead of the \nprecipitation shield. As of now, the highest probability for \nany freezing rain is in the southern James River Valley of ND. \nWith westerly winds, afternoon highs should reach the mid 40s to\nlow 50s statewide, and wouldn't be surprised if some locations \nmainly in southwestern part of the state don't wind up a little \nwarmer and reach the mid 50s when all is said and done.\n\nAs the surface low churns eastward, a tightening pressure\ngradient will lead to windy conditions on Friday, especially in\nthe southwest. Winds could approach advisory level in the far\nsouthwest, though with the lack of strong CAA or pressure rises,\nthe NBM could be a bit overdone at the moment. A passing cold\nfrontal boundary Friday night into Saturday morning may maintain\nbreezy conditions overnight before Saturday becomes windy once \nagain. The pressure gradient will then loosen resulting in light\nwinds on Sunday.\n\nIn regard to temperatures this weekend and into the middle of\nnext week, overall they look to be seasonable, albeit often on\nthe warm side of 'near average.' For Saturday, this translates \nto highs in the low 30s to low 40s. After which, highs look \nmostly to be in the 30s Sunday and into the start of the next \nworkweek. Lows are forecast to drop into the 20s most nights, \nthough any night where winds go calm and skies clear will likely\nresult in an NBM forecast that is too warm.",
